## The Vision

**Every great project starts with an idea. ClientPulse is where I turn mine into code.**

Minecraft has been more than a game to me. Its clients, performance optimizations, user interfaces, and customization possibilities have inspired me to look beyond playing and start understanding how the software itself works.

ClientPulse is my journey into Minecraft client-side development using Java and Fabric.

My goal is to grow beyond writing programs that simply work. I want to learn how to design maintainable systems, build smooth user experiences, investigate performance problems, and develop features that feel purposeful.

I'm building this project as a practical portfolio to demonstrate my growth as a Java developer, with the long-term ambition of pursuing opportunities in Minecraft client development, including at Lunar Client.

**This isn't about pretending to know everything. It's about learning, building, improving, and proving what I can do.**

## What I'm Building

ClientPulse is intended to grow into a modular Minecraft client-side project with a focus on useful in-game information, customization, and clean implementation.

### Planned Features

| Feature | Goal |
|---|---|
| Performance HUD | Display useful FPS and client performance information |
| Custom HUD | Allow on-screen elements to be positioned and configured |
| Feature management | Organize client-side features in a modular system |
| Keybind support | Make supported features easier to control |
| Settings interface | Provide a central place for configuration |
| Performance-minded design | Keep implementations practical and avoid unnecessary work |
| Clean architecture | Make future features easier to maintain and extend |

These are planned goals, not claims that the features already exist. Each will be marked as implemented after it works and has been tested.

## The Development Mindset

I want this repository to represent more than the final result. It should show how I approach software development.

- **Learn deeply:** Understand the Java concepts and APIs behind each feature.
- **Build deliberately:** Prefer clear architecture over rushed, tangled code.
- **Optimize intelligently:** Measure and investigate before making performance claims.
- **Test honestly:** Check changes in the development environment and fix problems.
- **Document progress:** Record meaningful milestones, decisions, and lessons.
- **Keep improving:** Treat bugs and setbacks as part of becoming a better developer.

